DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett() creates an anonymous RAM‐based file and returns  a  file
       descriptor  that  refers  to it.  The file provides a way to create and
       access memory regions with stronger  protection  than  usual  RAM‐based
       files  and  anonymous memory mappings.  Once all open references to the
       file are closed, it is automatically released.  The initial size of the
       file is set to 0.  Following the call, the file size should be set  us‐
       ing ffttrruunnccaattee(2).

       The memory areas backing the file created with mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett(2) are vis‐
       ible  only  to  the  processes that have access to the file descriptor.
       The memory region is removed from the kernel page tables and  only  the
       page tables of the processes holding the file descriptor map the corre‐
       sponding  physical memory.  (Thus, the pages in the region can’t be ac‐
       cessed by the kernel itself, so that, for example, pointers to the  re‐
       gion can’t be passed to system calls.)

       The following values may be bitwise ORed in _f_l_a_g_s to control the behav‐
       ior of mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett():

       FFDD__CCLLOOEEXXEECC
              Set  the  close‐on‐exec  flag  on the new file descriptor, which
              causes the region to be removed from the process  on  eexxeeccvvee(2).
              See the description of the OO__CCLLOOEEXXEECC flag in ooppeenn(2)

       As  its return value, mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett() returns a new file descriptor that
       refers to an anonymous file.  This file descriptor is opened  for  both
       reading  and  writing  (OO__RRDDWWRR) and OO__LLAARRGGEEFFIILLEE is set for the file de‐
       scriptor.

       With respect to ffoorrkk(2) and eexxeeccvvee(2), the usual  semantics  apply  for
       the  file descriptor created by mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett().  A copy of the file de‐
       scriptor is inherited by the child produced by ffoorrkk(2)  and  refers  to
       the  same file.  The file descriptor is preserved across eexxeeccvvee(2), un‐
       less the close‐on‐exec flag has been set.

       The memory region is locked  into  memory  in  the  same  way  as  with
       mmlloocckk(2),  so  that it will never be written into swap, and hibernation
       is inhibited for as long  as  any  mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett()  descriptions  exist.
       However  the  implementation of mmeemmffdd__sseeccrreett() will not try to populate
       the whole range during the mmmmaapp(2) call that attaches the  region  into
       the process’s address space; instead, the pages are only actually allo‐
       cated  as they are faulted in.  The amount of memory allowed for memory
       mappings of the file descriptor obeys the same rules  as  mmlloocckk(2)  and
       cannot exceed RRLLIIMMIITT__MMEEMMLLOOCCKK.

